{"text": "Here's the limit. A sensor the size of a fingernail simply can't collect much light in one go. Leave the shutter open longer, and anything that moves turns to blur. Turn up the amplification, and you amplify the noise right along with the picture. One exposure is always a compromise. So the phone doesn't take one. From the moment you open the camera, it's already shooting, frame after frame, into a small rolling buffer, keeping the last few and throwing the oldest away. Many of them are short exposures, that freeze motion and protect the highlights. When you tap the shutter, you're not really taking a picture. You're telling the phone which moment you meant. It keeps the frames around that instant, and adds a longer exposure to dig detail out of the shadows. In very dark scenes, it keeps collecting for a second or more. Now it has a stack of frames of the same scene, and none of them quite line up, because your hand moved, and so did the world. So the chip aligns them, shifting and warping each one, patch by patch, until every edge sits on top of itself. Anything that moved too much to match gets left out. Then they're merged. The scene is the same in every frame, but the noise is random, different every time. Average the frames, and the picture reinforces itself, while the noise cancels out. Nine frames, about a third of the noise. Machine learning models, running on the neural engine, decide pixel by pixel which frame to trust: the short ones for highlights, the long one for shadows, the sharpest for texture. The result is a photo that no single exposure from this sensor could have produced. It's not faking the scene. It's measuring it many times, and keeping the best of every measurement.", "alignment": {"characters": ["H", "e", "r", "e", "'", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "l", "i", "m", "i", "t", ".", " ", "A", " ", "s", "e", "n", "s", "o", "r", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "i", "z", "e", " ", "o", "f", " ", "a", " ", "f", "i", "n", "g", "e", "r", "n"